# Test-only configuration. Copy to `.env.test` (gitignored) — never to `.env`.
#
# `npm test` never reads the application's `.env`, and ignores any DATABASE_URL
# / REDIS_URL exported in your shell. The only database and Redis a test run can
# touch are the ones below, and only if they pass a safety check first:
#   - the database host must be loopback (localhost, 127.0.0.1 or ::1)
#   - the database name must contain "test" as a separate word (womenchat_test)
#   - Redis must be plain redis:// on loopback and use a dedicated logical
#     database from 1 to 15 (never database 0, where local dev data lives)
#   - ALLOW_TEST_DB_WRITES=1 must be set (explicit opt-in)
# Hosted databases (Neon, Upstash, …) are always rejected, whatever they contain.
#
# One-time local setup with the repo's docker-compose services:
#   docker compose up -d
#   docker compose exec postgres psql -U womenchat -d postgres -c "CREATE DATABASE womenchat_test;"
#   npm run test:db:migrate --workspace=apps/server     # applies migrations to TEST_DATABASE_URL only
# Then:
#   npm test --workspace=apps/server
#
# Tests that need no database or Redis (config validation, the simulate route,
# …) run without any of this: `npx vitest run src/config`.
ALLOW_TEST_DB_WRITES=1
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://womenchat:womenchat@localhost:5432/womenchat_test
TEST_REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/15
